{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "web": [
      {
        "title": "Going back to work after baby: Timelines and tips | BabyCenter",
        "url": "https://www.babycenter.com/baby/postpartum-health/when-can-i-go-back-to-work-after-delivery_1156149",
        "description": "As with most aspects of pregnancy and the postpartum period, there&#x27;s no one-size-fits-all rule on when to return to work after birth, and every new mom&#x27;s situation will be different. Some moms return to work just a few weeks after having a baby, while others take up to a year (or longer) of maternity leave.",
        "position": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Returning to work after maternity leave - Mayo Clinic Health System",
        "url": "https://www.mayoclinichealthsystem.org/hometown-health/speaking-of-health/returning-to-work-after-maternity-leave",
        "description": "<strong>Touch base with your employer a few weeks before you return to ensure they are prepared for your time and space needs</strong>. Ask if you can visit so you can visualize and plan where you will be pumping.",
        "position": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Work Reentry After Childbirth: Predictors of Self-Rated Health in Month One Among a Sample of University Faculty and Staff - PMC",
        "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7078554/",
        "description": "Regardless of the provision, many women in the United States return to work much sooner than twelve weeks: <strong>more than 25% return within two months of giving birth</strong> and about 10% return in four weeks or less [33]. In terms of the broader global context in which these patterns ensue, the United ...",
        "position": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Returning to Work - 4th Trimester Project",
        "url": "https://newmomhealth.com/selfcare/returning-to-work/",
        "description": "Needs slowly shift over time after ... responsibilities gradually, that can help with the transition. <strong>Starting mid-week can be helpful, so you navigate a few days before a break</strong>....",
        "position": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "When to Return to Work After Giving Birth",
        "url": "https://www.babymed.com/daily-living/when-work-after-giving-birth",
        "description": "These changes can cause extreme fatigue, in addition to the adjustment of having a new infant in the home. Most women wait about six weeks from their baby’s birth date to return to work, though some doctors will clear a new mother for work as early as three weeks postpartum.",
        "position": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Tips for Planning Your Return to Work After Parental Leave",
        "url": "https://www.thebump.com/a/returning-to-work-parental-leave",
        "description": "Buy or rent the equipment you’ll need and give it some test runs at least a couple of weeks before you return. Remember that the law requires employers to have adequate breaks and a space (aside from restrooms) to enable new moms to pump at work, says Abbey H., a mom of four. Consider working with a lactation professional or postpartum doula, she adds, “to learn how to use your pump effectively.”",
        "position": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "10 Tips for Going Back to Work After Baby",
        "url": "https://www.whattoexpect.com/first-year/returning-to-work-after-maternity-leave-emotions",
        "description": "Even in the very best of circumstances — perhaps you were lucky enough to take as much time off as you wanted, or you can&#x27;t wait to return to your job — going back to work can be challenging for many women. There are logistics to put in place, like child care, coordinating schedules with your partner and making a plan for pumping.",
        "position": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Returning to Work After Having a Baby | Americord Registry",
        "url": "https://www.americordblood.com/articles/returning-to-work-after-having-a-baby",
        "description": "Start by setting a firm return timeline, meeting with your employer to discuss accommodations (like flexible hours or a private pumping space), and practicing your new daily routine weeks in advance. Know your legal rights under the FMLA, the PUMP Act, and the PWFA regarding leave and lactation support, and build a strong support network of family, friends, and other working parents to manage the emotional and physical adjustments. Going back to work after having a baby can feel overwhelming, but with the right plan, it’s manageable. Many mothers face challenges like emotional struggles, physical recovery, and logistical hurdles, such as finding childcare or managing breastfeeding at work.",
        "position": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "r/NewParents on Reddit: Mothers who went back to work 8 weeks or less after giving birth, what was your experience?",
        "url": "https://www.reddit.com/r/NewParents/comments/f24zyx/mothers_who_went_back_to_work_8_weeks_or_less/",
        "description": "At the time, I felt my own pressure to go back being the new head of a department (I got the position at ~12 weeks pregnant) and not sure how I’d feel postpartum. For my second I’m taking 11-12 weeks. 6 weeks is doable but short and I don’t know if I could have done it without bringing my baby with me when I went back. They’re still so little and you’re still adjusting. I try to put it in perspective that 12 weeks is also short and a job is a job but you’ll have the rest of your work life post baby to work. ... I was personally ready to go back at about 10 weeks after my 2nd baby, a planned c-section.",
        "position": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "How to Manage Postpartum Back-To-Work Anxiety – Needed",
        "url": "https://thisisneeded.com/blogs/postpartum/how-to-manage-anxiety-around-returning-to-work-postpartum",
        "description": "If not, consider starting an informal support network, weekly coffee chats, or monthly check-ins can make a huge difference. Remember: you don’t have to navigate this alone. The logistics of drop-offs, pickups, sick days, and household chores can quickly create friction if left unaddressed.",
        "position": 10
      }
    ]
  }
}

